-`MarkdownCore` has no UI dependency. `MarkdownRender` owns AppKit rendering.
+`MarkdownCore` has no UI dependency. `MarkdownRender` owns rendering, in two
+halves:
+
+- **Shared engine:** the engine, fragments, themes, syntax and TextKit 2
+  content storage build for macOS and iOS. They name colours, fonts, images
+  and appearances through the aliases in `MarkdownRender/Platform/Platform.swift`
+  (`PlatformColor`, `PlatformFont`, `PlatformImage`, `PlatformAppearance`).
+  On macOS each alias is the AppKit type, so the Mac build is unchanged. Where
+  the two SDKs differ, shared code uses `#if os(macOS)` or a helper in that
+  file. On iOS the file also adds the few AppKit-only methods the shared code
+  calls.
+- **View layer:** `View/` and `Motion.swift` are AppKit-only and compile out
+  on iOS. The exceptions are `MarkdownContentStorage`, `ParagraphSubstitution`
+  and `FragmentProvider`, which are shared.
+- **iOS reading surface:** `UIKitView/MarkdownReaderView` is a read-only
+  `UITextView` on TextKit 2. It builds the same surface from the same shared
+  pieces as `MarkdownTextView`:
+  - content storage and paragraph substitution;
+  - `DecorationEngine`;
+  - `Engine/PresentationMaps` (the read-mode display map, shared with the Mac
+    view);
+  - `FragmentProvider` and the fragments.
+  It has no editing, caret reveal or folding.
+  - Links route through `UITextViewDelegate` to `linkHandler`.
+  - Navigation (the outline, `ReaderOutlineController`, and the VoiceOver
+    heading and link rotors) is in `MarkdownReaderView+Navigation`.
+  - It sets its own scroll content height, as the Mac view sets its frame.
+  See `IOS-PORT-PLAN.md`.
+- **iOS app:** `iOSApp/` (its own XcodeGen spec, outside the Mac project) is
+  a read-only document-browser app.
+  - It decodes with `DocumentIO` and never writes: `MarkdownDocument`
+    refuses to produce contents.
+  - It reloads on coordinated outside edits and follows renames.
+  - It keeps the last text, with a notice, when the file is deleted.
+
 The app owns windows, file access, and user actions. Quick Look uses the same
 core and render contracts as the app. The Spotlight targets share only the
 parser-backed metadata contract and never start an AppKit surface.
